<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#khedive-of-egypt">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khedive of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Khedive</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khedive was the title of the hereditary viceroys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Ottoman suzerainty, granted by the sultan to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Isma'il Pasha</a> in 1867 and used until 1914. The khedives sought to build an African empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> and towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a>, employing Europeans such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-baker">Samuel Baker</a> and Charles Gordon. After the British occupation of 1882, real power passed to British officials, and in 1914 the title was replaced by sultan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Khedive of Egypt" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Converted to svg by Daniel78 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Egypt_(1882%E2%80%931922).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Khedive, a term of Persian origin, was the title that distinguished the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from other Ottoman governors, called walis. Muhammad Ali, an Ottoman officer, was recognised as governor of Egypt from 1805 to 1848, established de facto autonomy from the sultan, and by 1841 secured recognition of his descendants' right to hold the office of viceroy. His successors were his son Ibrahim, his grandson Abbas I, his son Muhammad Sa'id, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Isma'il Pasha</a>, a son of Ibrahim, who took the throne in 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A firman of 8 June 1867 granted Isma'il the title of khedive from the Ottoman sultan Abdulaziz. It came with near-complete control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s internal affairs and the right to negotiate certain international agreements. In return for doubling his tributary payments to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, Isma'il also won the right to change the order of succession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a>. A second firman, of 8 June 1873, let him take foreign loans and removed limits on the size of the army. Egypt was not independent: it remained an autonomous tributary state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Three men held the title. Isma'il (1863–1879) pursued European-style modernisation and sought to expand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s rule along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and the African coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, but his army was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> at the Battles of Gundet in 1875 and Gura in 1876. His costly projects brought heavy foreign debt, and the European powers pressed the sultan to depose him, which happened on 29 June 1879. His son Tawfiq (1879–1892) saw European influence reimposed and the Urabi revolution suppressed when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> invaded in 1882. The British then took control of the country, with Tawfiq restored.</p><p>Abbas II (1892–1914) grew hostile to the British. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> proclaimed a Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and abolished the Khedivate on 5 November 1914. Abbas II, on a state visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, was deposed in his absence and banned from returning. His uncle Hussein Kamel took the title of sultan on 19 December 1914.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khedivate of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 1860s he had proposed to Isma'il Pasha, <b>Khedive of Egypt</b>, a huge lighthouse in the form of a robed peasant woman holding a torch at the entrance to the Suez Canal; the khedive and Ferdinand de Lesseps declined it because of the cost.</blockquote><a href="/work/statue-of-liberty">Statue of Liberty · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The first, known as the &quot;<b>Khedive</b>'s Special Salute&quot; or &quot;Salam Affandina&quot;, dated from the reign of Khedive Ismail: it was played at the opening of the Suez Canal and adopted in 1871, an instrumental piece without official words whose most likely author was Giuseppe Pugioli, an Italian cornetist…</blockquote><a href="/songs/biladi-biladi-biladi">Biladi, Biladi, Biladi</a></li></ul></section>
